MQTT (Message Queuing Telemetry Transport) is a lightweight, publish-subscribe network
protocol designed for resource-constrained devices and low-bandwidth, high-latency, or
unreliable networks. It is a gold standard for IoT (Internet of Things) and machine-to-machine
(M2M) communication.
Core Concepts
● Publish/Subscribe Architecture: Unlike traditional client-server models (like HTTP),
MQTT decouples the sender of a message (publisher) from the receiver (subscriber)
using a message broker.
● The Broker: The central hub (e.g., Mosquitto, HiveMQ) that filters all incoming messages
and distributes them to the correct subscribed clients.
● Topics: UTF-8 strings used by the broker to filter messages for each connected client.
Topics are structured hierarchically with slashes (e.g., home/livingroom/temperature).
● Clients: Any device (from a microcontroller like an ESP32 to a cloud server) that runs an
MQTT library and connects to the broker to publish or subscribe.
Key Features
● Lightweight & Efficient: Features a minimal header size (as small as 2 bytes),
minimizing network traffic and conserving battery power on remote sensors.
● Quality of Service (QoS): Offers three levels of delivery assurance:
○ QoS 0 (At most once): Fire and forget (messages may be lost).
○ QoS 1 (At least once): Guaranteed delivery, but duplicates may occur.
○ QoS 2 (Exactly once): Guaranteed delivery with no duplicates (slowest).
● Last Will and Testament (LWT): Allows a client to notify the broker beforehand of a
pre-set message to broadcast automatically if the client disconnects unexpectedly.
● Retained Messages: The broker can store the last message sent on a topic so that
newly connected subscribers immediately receive the latest state without waiting for a
new publish event.
Common Use Cases
● Smart Home Automation: Devices like lights, thermostats, and smart plugs
communicating locally or via cloud hubs (e.g., Home Assistant).
● Industrial IoT (IIoT): Monitoring heavy machinery performance, pipeline pressures, and
factory floor telemetry.
● Logistics & Tracking: Fleet management systems reporting real-time vehicle locations
and cargo conditions.
